// The ISO 3166-1 alpha-2 country codes, their English names, and which time
// zones each one uses.
//
// Any program that takes an address, prices something, ships something or
// shows a local time needs to turn `DE` into "Germany" and `DE` into
// `Europe/Berlin`. Neither mapping is derivable and both change — Czechia
// became `CZ` in 1993, South Sudan appeared in 2011, the zone list moves
// every year — so the only correct version is one taken from a source that
// is maintained.
//
// This table comes from the time zone database's `iso3166.tab` and
// `zone.tab`, which are in the public domain, are updated several times a
// year, and are on every machine that can tell the time. That is also what
// the gate checks against: it re-reads those files and compares, so an
// update to the system's tzdata shows up as a failing gate rather than as
// quietly stale data here.
//
// ## What is deliberately missing
//
// The alpha-3 and numeric codes. ISO sells the standard that defines them
// and no table on a normal machine carries them, so writing them here would
// mean writing 249 three-letter codes from memory — which is exactly the
// kind of invention that has already been caught four times in this project.
// `alpha3_of` does not exist rather than existing and being wrong.
//
// The names here are the tz database's, which depart from ISO's in a few
// places on purpose: "Samoa (American)" rather than "American Samoa" so that
// a sorted list groups them, and "Turkey" rather than "Türkiye" to avoid
// churn. The file says so itself, and this follows the file.

fn packed_names_0() -> str {
    return "AD=Andorra|AE=United Arab Emirates|AF=Afghanistan|" +
    "AG=Antigua & Barbuda|AI=Anguilla|AL=Albania|AM=Armenia|" +
    "AO=Angola|AQ=Antarctica|AR=Argentina|AS=Samoa (American)|" +
    "AT=Austria|AU=Australia|AW=Aruba|AX=Åland Islands|" +
    "AZ=Azerbaijan|BA=Bosnia & Herzegovina|BB=Barbados|" +
    "BD=Bangladesh|BE=Belgium|BF=Burkina Faso|BG=Bulgaria|" +
    "BH=Bahrain|BI=Burundi|BJ=Benin|"
}

